+# CSV import
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one. The import must tell them exactly what happened:
+which contacts were created, which were updated, and which rows were rejected and why,
+so they can fix the spreadsheet and re-import it.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`, tags joined
+with `;`). Files coming from the address-book export, which has `first_name` and
+`last_name` instead of `name`, must also be accepted.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+`import_contacts`, `ImportReport`, `RowIssue` and `ImportFileError` are also re-exported
+from the `contactbook` package.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Decoding
+
+1. `bytes` input is decoded as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM is accepted and ignored.
+2. `str` input with a leading BOM character (U+FEFF) is accepted the same way. The BOM
+   is never part of the first column name.
+3. Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`. The message tells the user to
+   re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+4. Only comma-delimited files are supported. Fields may be quoted the usual CSV way,
+   including commas and line breaks inside quotes. `\n` and `\r\n` line endings both work.
+
+### Row numbering
+
+5. `RowIssue.row` is the row number a spreadsheet shows when it opens the file. The
+   header is row 1 and the first contact row is row 2. Every row counts, including blank
+   rows. A quoted cell that spans several lines is still one row.
+
+### Header
+
+6. The first row is the header, and a header is required. Column names are matched
+   case-insensitively, surrounding spaces are ignored, and columns can be in any order.
+7. Required columns are `email` and a name. The name is either a `name` column, or both
+   a `first_name` column and a `last_name` column. Optional columns are `phone` and `tags`.
+8. If `email` is missing, or there's no name source (no `name` and not both `first_name`
+   and `last_name`), `ImportFileError` is raised and the message names the missing column.
+9. **[decision]** If `name` is present, it is used, and `first_name`/`last_name` are
+   treated as unknown columns.
+10. Unknown columns never cause an error. Each one produces one warning with `row=1`
+    and `column=<the column name as written>`, and its values are ignored.
+11. **[decision]** A header that names the same column twice (after case and space
+    normalisation) raises `ImportFileError`, because the value to use would be ambiguous.
+
+### Whole-file rejections (nothing is imported)
+
+12. A completely empty file (no bytes or characters at all), or one with nothing but
+    blank lines, raises `ImportFileError`.
+13. A file with a header and no contact rows is not an error. It returns an empty report.
+14. A file with more than **1000** contact rows raises `ImportFileError`. Blank rows don't
+    count toward the limit. Exactly 1000 contact rows is allowed.
+15. When `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is left exactly as it was.
+16. **[decision]** CSV the parser can't read at all (e.g. a single cell larger than 128 KiB) also raises
+    `ImportFileError`.
+
+### Rows
+
+17. Blank rows are skipped silently and don't appear in the report. A row is blank if it
+    is an empty line or every cell is empty or only spaces.
+18. Each non-blank row is validated with the existing validators (`validation.py` /
+    `Contact.create`). Emails are normalised (trimmed, lower-cased).
+19. With `first_name`/`last_name`, the name is the two values joined with one space, then
+    normalised like any name. Either part may be blank, but not both.
+20. Tags: the cell is split on `;`. Each piece is normalised with the existing tag helper.
+    Empty pieces and repeats are ignored, so `"VIP; ;vip;Key Account"` gives
+    `{vip, key-account}`.
+21. An invalid value rejects the whole row. That row gets an error with `column` set to
+    `name`, `email`, `phone` or `tags`, and a message saying what's wrong.
+    **[decision]** If several cells in one row are invalid, each produces its own error.
+    With `first_name`/`last_name`, a name error is reported on column `name`.
+22. A rejected row isn't applied at all, not even partly. Other rows still import
+    (partial import).
+23. **[decision]** A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as
+    empty. A row with extra non-empty cells beyond the header is rejected with an error
+    (`column=None`). Extra empty cells are ignored.
+24. Duplicate emails in one file: the first row with a given (normalised) email claims
+    it. Every later row with that email is an error on the later row (`column="email"`)
+    that names the earlier row, and it isn't applied. **[decision]** This applies even
+    when the first row was itself rejected for another reason.
+
+### Applying rows
+
+25. A valid row whose email isn't in the repository creates a contact. Its normalised
+    email is appended to `created`.
+26. A valid row whose email is already in the repository (compared case-insensitively)
+    is merged into the existing contact:
+    - the name from the file replaces the stored name;
+    - the phone is replaced only if the phone cell has a value; an empty cell or a missing
+      `phone` column never clears an existing phone;
+    - the file's tags are added to the existing tags; an import never removes a tag.
+27. The normalised email of a merged contact is appended to `updated` only if the merge
+    changed something. **[decision]** An existing contact that the row leaves identical
+    appears in neither `created` nor `updated`.
+28. `created`, `updated`, `errors` and `warnings` are in file order.
+29. Rows are only applied once the whole file has been checked for file-level problems,
+    so criterion 15 holds.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Delimiters other than comma (e.g. semicolon "European Excel" CSV), and encodings
+  other than UTF-8 (no guessing of cp1252, UTF-16 etc.).
+- Removing contacts, clearing phones or removing tags via import.
+- Spreadsheet formats other than CSV (xlsx, ods).
+- Files larger than 1000 contact rows (they're rejected, not split or streamed).
+- A CLI, HTTP endpoint or `ContactBook` method for import. Callers use `import_contacts`.
+- Dry-run/preview mode, undo, and all-or-nothing import of row-level errors.
+- Persisting the report or the original file.
